{"insertText":{"location":{"index":144719,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"text":"\nRQ1 also concerns accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. Table X gives these metrics at the frozen source thresholds, and the ROC curves in the figure after it show where each frozen threshold fell on the target curve."}},
{"updateParagraphStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144720,"endIndex":144943,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"paragraphStyle":{"namedStyleType":"NORMAL_TEXT","alignment":"JUSTIFIED","lineSpacing":200,"indentFirstLine":{"magnitude":36,"unit":"PT"},"spaceAbove":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"spaceBelow":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"keepWithNext":false},"fields":"namedStyleType,alignment,lineSpacing,indentFirstLine,spaceAbove,spaceBelow,keepWithNext"}},
{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144720,"endIndex":144942,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144942,"endIndex":144943,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"insertText":{"location":{"index":144719,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"text":"\nThreshold-Dependent Metrics Under Transfer"}},
{"updateParagraphStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144720,"endIndex":144763,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"paragraphStyle":{"namedStyleType":"NORMAL_TEXT","alignment":"JUSTIFIED","lineSpacing":200,"indentFirstLine":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"spaceAbove":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"spaceBelow":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"keepWithNext":true},"fields":"namedStyleType,alignment,lineSpacing,indentFirstLine,spaceAbove,spaceBelow,keepWithNext"}},
{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144720,"endIndex":144762,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"},"bold":true},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144762,"endIndex":144763,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"},"bold":true},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"insertText":{"location":{"index":144719,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"text":"\nThe significant differences were stable across seeds and analysis choices. Nine of the ten had the same sign in all nine pairings of individual seeds, and the tenth (Celeb-DF v2 → FF++ C40 against Video Swin-B) in eight of nine; all ten remained significant under the family grouping recorded before scoring and under one pooled family, and no DeeperForensics-1.0-target decision changed with 175 lineage clusters (Appendix J, Table J7). Two limits apply. Transfer lowered ROC-AUC for every model, to between 0.459 and 0.976, and from DeeperForensics-1.0 to Celeb-DF v2 all three models were near chance (0.4593 to 0.6001). The three significant differences from DeeperForensics-1.0 to FF++ C23 and C40 could not be checked meaningfully on a lineage-free subset, because only 20 such test videos exist (Table VI; per-seed values in Table J3). Under matched training, SwinFusion++ therefore ranked target videos significantly better than Swin-B in seven of the nine transfer directions and than Video Swin-B in three. Whether these differences can be attributed to TLBG-HCA, rather than to the combination of two encoders, is examined under RQ4 with the topology and capacity-matched controls."}},
{"updateParagraphStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144720,"endIndex":145913,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"paragraphStyle":{"namedStyleType":"NORMAL_TEXT","alignment":"JUSTIFIED","lineSpacing":200,"indentFirstLine":{"magnitude":36,"unit":"PT"},"spaceAbove":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"spaceBelow":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"keepWithNext":false},"fields":"namedStyleType,alignment,lineSpacing,indentFirstLine,spaceAbove,spaceBelow,keepWithNext"}},
{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144720,"endIndex":145912,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":145912,"endIndex":145913,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"insertText":{"location":{"index":144719,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"text":"\nThe advantage was clearest when the models were trained on Celeb-DF v2 or DeeperForensics-1.0: from these sources, SwinFusion++ was significantly better than Swin-B in all six directions and than Video Swin-B in three. From FF++ C23, SwinFusion++ was statistically indistinguishable from both encoders on FF++ C40 and Celeb-DF v2, and the one significant difference was the largest of all 18: on DeeperForensics-1.0, Swin-B’s ROC-AUC fell to 0.5678 [0.5250, 0.6105], while the two models with a temporal stream kept 0.8261 (SwinFusion++, +0.258 over Swin-B) and 0.8421 (Video Swin-B). The same ordering appeared on the 60-video subset that shares no source lineage with the FF++ C23 training or validation data (seed means: Swin-B 0.5322, SwinFusion++ 0.7356, Video Swin-B 0.7874; no interval; Appendix J, Table J3). This pattern is consistent with temporal evidence transferring better than frame appearance in this direction. Swin-B and Video Swin-B also differ in pretraining and input handling, however, so the comparison does not isolate the cause."}},
{"updateParagraphStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144720,"endIndex":145774,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"paragraphStyle":{"namedStyleType":"NORMAL_TEXT","alignment":"JUSTIFIED","lineSpacing":200,"indentFirstLine":{"magnitude":36,"unit":"PT"},"spaceAbove":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"spaceBelow":{"magnitude":0,"unit":"PT"},"keepWithNext":false},"fields":"namedStyleType,alignment,lineSpacing,indentFirstLine,spaceAbove,spaceBelow,keepWithNext"}},
{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":144720,"endIndex":145773,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"updateTextStyle":{"range":{"startIndex":145773,"endIndex":145774,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"textStyle":{"fontSize":{"magnitude":12,"unit":"PT"}},"fields":"bold,italic,underline,backgroundColor,fontSize"}},
{"insertText":{"location":{"index":144719,"tabId":"t.l5c18frq5wuo"},"text":"\nUnder frozen transfer, SwinFusion++ had a significantly higher ROC-AUC than Swin-B in seven of the nine directions, by +0.049 to +0.258 (Holm-adjusted p from < 0.005 to 0.018), and than Video Swin-B in three, Celeb-DF v2 → FF++ C40 (+0.0754), Celeb-DF v2 → DeeperForensics-1.0 (+0.0630), and DeeperForensics-1.0 → FF++ C40 (+0.0840), all with Holm-adjusted p < 0.005. In these three directions, it was significantly better than both encoders and had the highest ROC-AUC of the three models. SwinFusion++ was significantly lower than an encoder in none of the 18 comparisons. This is not evidence of non-inferiority: the five negative point estimates were not significant, but the widest interval reached −0.092 (DeeperForensics-1.0 → Celeb-DF v2, against Video Swin-B). SwinFusion++ also had the highest point estimate in FF++ C23 → C40 and DeeperForensics-1.0 → FF++ C23 (heatmap), but its lead over the runner-up, Video Swin-B, was not significant in either direction (+0.0014; and +0.0451 [+0.0077, +0.0816], Holm-adjusted p = 0.152, shown with an open marker in the forest plot)."}},
